WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.157
Hello, and welcome to In Simple Terms with Satish.

00:00:03.157 --> 00:00:07.092
Have you ever opened an AI model card and noticed two sizes:

00:00:07.092 --> 00:00:10.509
total parameters and active parameters per token?

00:00:10.509 --> 00:00:12.976
You download one model and receive one answer.

00:00:12.976 --> 00:00:15.419
So why does it need two parameter counts?

00:00:15.419 --> 00:00:20.932
That puzzle leads to an architecture called Mixture of Experts, or MoE.

00:00:20.932 --> 00:00:22.453
Picture a busy food court.

00:00:22.453 --> 00:00:25.847
In one setup, every order goes through one large kitchen.

00:00:25.847 --> 00:00:31.637
That resembles a dense Transformer: inside each block, every token—“compiler,” “coffee,” or

00:00:31.637 --> 00:00:34.510
a comma—passes through the same feed-forward network.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:38.064
Now imagine eight cooking stations and a digital dispatcher.

00:00:38.064 --> 00:00:42.470
An order ticket contains enough detail for the dispatcher to score every

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:45.000
station, choose the top two and blend

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.799
their contributions into the final tray.

00:00:46.799 --> 00:00:49.096
In the model, the ticket is the

00:00:49.096 --> 00:00:51.731
token's hidden vector, shaped by its context.

00:00:51.731 --> 00:00:54.052
The dispatcher is a learned router.

00:00:54.052 --> 00:00:56.934
The stations are expert feed-forward subnetworks.

00:00:56.934 --> 00:01:00.504
The final tray is the routing-weighted combination of their outputs.

00:01:00.504 --> 00:01:04.373
The other six stations do no feed-forward calculation for that token.

00:01:04.373 --> 00:01:05.812
The analogy has a boundary.

00:01:05.812 --> 00:01:09.960
Food stations may carry fixed labels such as “grill” or “dessert.” Neural

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.557
experts are not assigned human-readable jobs.

00:01:12.557 --> 00:01:17.514
During training they learn overlapping roles, and the same written word can

00:01:17.514 --> 00:01:20.111
reach a different pair when its context changes.

00:01:20.111 --> 00:01:24.209
The food court helps us picture selective routing; it does not tell

00:01:24.209 --> 00:01:28.203
us what an expert “knows.” That selective routing is sparse activation.

00:01:28.203 --> 00:01:32.511
The model still owns the weights for all eight experts, so they

00:01:32.511 --> 00:01:36.086
contribute to its total parameter count and must be stored somewhere.

00:01:36.086 --> 00:01:40.514
But only the selected two contribute to that token's expert calculation.

00:01:40.514 --> 00:01:45.703
Add the always-used attention layers, embeddings, and other shared components, and you

00:01:45.703 --> 00:01:48.056
get the model's active parameters per token.

00:01:48.056 --> 00:01:51.594
To see this in a current model, Hugging Face's 2026 guide gives

00:01:51.594 --> 00:01:57.317
gpt-oss-20b as an example: about 21 billion total parameters, four of 32

00:01:57.317 --> 00:02:01.170
experts selected, and about 3.6 billion active parameters

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.960
per token after shared components are included.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.679
That figure does not predict exact speed,

00:02:06.679 --> 00:02:08.869
but it makes the distinction visible.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:09.862
This distinction matters.

00:02:09.862 --> 00:02:14.409
Total parameters describe the model's overall capacity and storage footprint.

00:02:14.409 --> 00:02:16.994
Active parameters are closer to the amount

00:02:16.994 --> 00:02:19.103
of arithmetic performed for one token.

00:02:19.103 --> 00:02:21.834
MoE aims to expand the first without

00:02:21.834 --> 00:02:24.432
increasing the second at the same rate.

00:02:24.432 --> 00:02:27.102
But the router creates a new engineering problem.

00:02:27.102 --> 00:02:29.894
What if it keeps choosing the same two experts?

00:02:29.894 --> 00:02:33.790
Those experts become crowded while others receive too little training.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:37.192
During serving, popular experts can also create queues.

00:02:37.192 --> 00:02:42.804
MoE systems therefore use techniques such as balancing losses, routing constraints, or

00:02:42.804 --> 00:02:45.784
capacity limits to spread work more effectively.

00:02:45.784 --> 00:02:49.512
The exact approach varies, and perfect balance can conflict

00:02:49.512 --> 00:02:52.531
with routing each token where the model prefers.

00:02:52.531 --> 00:02:55.340
There is another cost hiding between the experts.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.403
Large models often place different experts on different accelerators.

00:02:59.403 --> 00:03:01.725
This is expert parallelism.

00:03:01.725 --> 00:03:05.374
After routing, token representations must travel to

00:03:05.374 --> 00:03:07.628
the devices holding the chosen experts.

00:03:07.628 --> 00:03:11.684
Their results must then return to the correct positions in the sequence.

00:03:11.684 --> 00:03:15.092
That exchange can involve expensive all-to-all communication.

00:03:15.092 --> 00:03:19.889
So fewer active weights do not automatically produce a faster response.

00:03:19.889 --> 00:03:24.567
If batches are small, routes are uneven, kernels are inefficient, or the

00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:27.736
network between accelerators is slow, communication and memory

00:03:27.736 --> 00:03:31.172
movement can erase part of the computational advantage.

00:03:31.172 --> 00:03:36.632
The full expert weights may also require substantial accelerator memory even though

00:03:36.632 --> 00:03:38.667
each token uses only a subset.

00:03:38.667 --> 00:03:42.775
MoE is therefore not several complete models debating an answer.

00:03:42.775 --> 00:03:47.753
It is conditional computation inside one trained model: a router chooses which

00:03:47.753 --> 00:03:50.226
learned feed-forward paths process each token.

00:03:50.226 --> 00:03:54.054
When you see an MoE model advertised, ask four questions.

00:03:54.054 --> 00:03:56.348
How many parameters exist in total?

00:03:56.348 --> 00:03:58.396
How many are active per token?

00:03:58.396 --> 00:04:00.877
How many experts does the router select?

00:04:00.877 --> 00:04:05.087
And what hardware and serving system are required to run it efficiently?

00:04:05.087 --> 00:04:07.969
Those answers reveal far more than the

00:04:07.969 --> 00:04:09.610
largest number in the model name.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.843
Which AI term should we unpack next:

00:04:11.843 --> 00:04:15.135
routing, active parameters, or expert parallelism?

00:04:15.135 --> 00:04:16.490
Tell me in the comments.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:19.805
If this helped you understand the topic in simple terms, follow or

00:04:19.805 --> 00:04:22.516
subscribe to In Simple Terms with Satish, and share

00:04:22.516 --> 00:04:25.206
this episode with one friend who likes technology.

00:04:25.206 --> 00:04:27.080
I will see you in the next episode.
